e, matching the expected process exactly -- no unplanned step_ids were seen in this run. Executor reported run_ended status=completed with all 12 planned steps successful.\n\nWhat I observed: for each part I armed an effort_event monitor (threshold = that object's raw_spec.limits.max_clamp_effort, contract clause invariants.grasp_effort_instantaneous) and a relative_frame monitor between tcp and the held object (threshold = raw_spec.tolerances.grasp_slip_tolerance_m, instruction-level in-hand-slippage invariant) for the duration each part was held; neither ever fired for any of the four parts, so clamp effort stayed within each part's registry-tolerated limit and no in-hand slip occurred. A stationarity monitor (threshold = raw_spec.tolerances.disturbance_tolerance_m = 0.02 m, or agent-chosen 0.02 m for base1/bystanders which carry no registry contract) was kept armed continuously over base1, all five bystander objects, and every task part not currently being worked; none fired, so nothing outside each step's declared object_ids moved at any point.\n\nAt each part's own place step close (its milestone), I independently queried that part's pose and its placement frame via query_snapshot/get_evidence (not the executor's claim) and computed the position error myself: u_brown 0.0022 m (tol 0.01), u_orange 0.0024 m (tol 0.02), line_green 0.0069 m (tol 0.015), inverted_u_yellow 0.0012 m (tol 0.012) -- all comfortably within tolerance and matching the executor's self-reported numbers. Lift height (0.2-0.3 m, all >= the 0.05 m floor), grasp width error (<=0.0042 m against the 0.02 m plausibility band), and grasp effort plateau (8.8-32.1, all >= the 0.05 air-close floor) were confirmed from phase-event payloads and evidence for each pick, ruling out an air-grasp or wrong-feature grasp. At every milestone I also re-measured base1 and every already-seated part's drift from their last-known pose; the largest was ~4 mm, well inside the 0.02 m disturbance tolerance, so no seated part or the fixture was disturbed by later steps.\n\nAt run end I re-queried all four parts, their placement frames, base1, and all five bystanders one final time: every part is within tolerance of its placement frame, base1 and the five cross-assembly bystanders are at (within noise of) their exact run-start reference positions, confirming the ACM-permitted carried-part/bystander contact channel was never actually exploited to displace anything. An end-of-run `look` showed no unidentified object in the workspace and no evidence of an intruder.\n\nNothing I judged suspicious was left unflagged: no monitor ever fired, and every end_step evidence check independently corroborated the executor's claims rather than contradicting them, so there is no disagreement to report between the executor's claimed outcome and my own measurement here.\n\nOne coverage gap to disclose plainly: workspace observation beyond TF/stationarity was performed with a single `look` call at the very end of the run rather than periodically throughout, so a transient unregistered visual event that left no TF trace between my step/phase checkpoints would not have been caught by camera evidence (only by the continuous TF-based stationarity coverage, which did run throughout and did not fire). No such event's TF signature was ever observed.\n\nVerdict: conformant. All four end-states meet their placement tolerances, all invariants held for the whole run, and no violation stands.","run_scope":true,"executor_terminal_status":"completed","findings_during_run":[],"recovered":false},"subscriber_count":2,"seq":57}
